'''Vikas Minar''' (The Tower of Progress) is a 23-storey building in [[Delhi]] that houses offices of the [[Delhi Development Authority]]. Built in 1976, it was designed by [[Habib Rahman (architect)|Habib Rahman]] and was at completion the first building in Delhi to surpass the [[Qutb Minar]] in height.<ref>{{cite web|title=vikas minar, Feature Photo, Aerial view of the Vikas Minar...|url=https://www.timescontent.com/syndication-photos/reprint/feature/441885/vikas-minar-dda-delhi-development-a.html|website=Times Content|access-date=2022-05-07|archive-url=https://archive.today/20251127112115/https://web.archive.org/web/20220507164956/https://www.timescontent.com/syndication-photos/reprint/feature/441885/vikas-minar-dda-delhi-development-a.html|archive-date=27 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Roy|first=Sidharatha|title=Once Delhi's tallest building, iconic Vikas Minar is all set for a facelift|url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/delhi/once-citys-tallest-building-iconic-vikas-minar-is-all-set-for-a-facelift/articleshow/71797923.cms|access-date=2022-05-07|work=The Times of India|date=2019-10-29}}</ref>

==Design and construction==
Vikas Minar was designed by Habib Rahman, the then Chief Architect of the [[Central Public Works Department]], as a point-block tower and construction lasted from 1969 to 1976.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Lang|first1=Jon T.|title=A Concise History of Modern Architecture in India|date=2002|publisher=Orient Blackswan|isbn=978-81-7824-017-6|page=93|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gxyGbhlKQXQC&dq=%22vikas+minar%22&pg=PA93|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref> Actual construction was undertaken by the Ansal Group at a cost of {{INR}}85 lakhs.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Namburu|first1=Manoj|title=Moguls of Real Estate|date=2007-10-01|publisher=[[Roli Books Private Limited]]|isbn=978-93-5194-074-6|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QznBBwAAQBAJ&dq=%22vikas+minar%22+taller+than&pg=PT145|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Ansal Housing|url=https://www.ansals.com/corporate/corporate-about-us.asp|website=www.ansals.com|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref> At 82 meters in height, it was the [[List of tallest buildings in Delhi NCR|tallest building in Delhi]] and the first to surpass the Qutb Minar and became a [[landmark]] in Delhi.<ref>{{cite book|last=Versaci|first=Antonella|title=Conservation of Architectural Heritage (CAH): Embodiment of Identity|year=2022|publisher=[[Springer Nature]]|isbn=978-3-030-95564-9|page=294|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cjNtEAAAQBAJ&dq=%22vikas+minar%22&pg=PA294|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref><ref>[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/delhi/after-over-two-decades-ddas-vikas-minar-secures-fire-safety-clearance/articleshow/123218667.cms "After over two decades, DDA's Vikas Minar secures fire safety clearance"]. ''[https://wework.co.in/delhi/ WeWork]''. 10 August 2025. {{ISSN|0971-8257}}. Retrieved 2025-11-27.</ref> With its exposed [[Beam (structure)|beams]] and [[column]]s, the building, which is devoid of [[louver]]s, [[façade]]s or any decorative elements, is an important example of [[Modernist architecture]] in Delhi although its structure and design have been criticized as being visually unappealing and grossly [[Heating, ventilation, and air conditioning|energy inefficient]].<ref>{{cite book|last1=Akhtar|first1=S. M.|title=Habib Rahman: The Architect of Independent India|date=2017-10-16|publisher=Copal Publishing Group|isbn=978-93-83419-35-7|pages=47–48|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TkOdDwAAQBAJ&dq=%22vikas+minar%22&pg=PA46|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Lang|first1=Jon T.|title=A Concise History of Modern Architecture in India|date=2002|publisher=Orient Blackswan|isbn=978-81-7824-017-6|page=93|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gxyGbhlKQXQC&dq=%22vikas+minar%22&pg=PA93|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Hashmi|first1=Faizi O.|title=Beyond My Manor: A Life of My Choosing - Not Giving a Fig, Is Not the Best Option!|date=2015-06-15|publisher=Partridge Publishing|isbn=978-1-4828-4822-9}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Buch|first1=Mahesh N.|title=Environmental Consciousness and Urban Planning|date=1993|publisher=Orient Blackswan|isbn=978-0-86311-376-5|page=76|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_h66p0kof_gC&dq=%22vikas+minar%22&pg=PA76|access-date=2022-05-07}}</ref>

It was the tallest building in Delhi until Ambadeep (84 meters) and Hansalaya (88 meters) were built.<ref name="TOI_Facelift">{{cite news |last= Roy |first= Sidharatha |title= Once Delhi’s tallest building, iconic Vikas Minar is all set for a facelift |newspaper=The Times of India |location=New Delhi |publisher=The Times Group |date= 29 October 2019 |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/delhi/once-citys-tallest-building-iconic-vikas-minar-is-all-set-for-a-facelift/articleshow/71797923.cms |access-date=23 December 2025}}</ref>

